INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','easy','mcq','Cardiac muscle cells differ from skeletal muscle fibres in that they are connected end-to-end by specialised junctions. What are these junctions called?','["A. Intercalated discs", "B. Sarcomeres", "C. Motor end plates", "D. Gap junctions only"]','A','Cardiac myocytes are connected by intercalated discs, which contain gap junctions (nexuses) and desmosomes. Gap junctions provide low-resistance electrical pathways that allow action potentials to spread rapidly from cell to cell, making the heart function as a syncytium. Desmosomes mechanically hold the cells together during contraction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','medium','mcq','A 35-year-old medical student studies the cardiac action potential. Which ion is primarily responsible for the plateau phase (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential?','["A. Sodium ions entering via fast channels", "B. Calcium ions entering via L-type channels", "C. Potassium ions leaving via IK channels", "D. Chloride ions entering the cell"]','B','The plateau (phase 2) of the ventricular action potential lasts 200-300 ms and is sustained primarily by calcium influx through L-type (long-lasting) voltage-gated calcium channels. This prolonged depolarisation prevents premature re-excitation and allows complete ventricular contraction before the next beat. The calcium influx also triggers calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','medium','mcq','The resting membrane potential of a ventricular myocyte is approximately -90 mV. Which ion primarily maintains this resting potential?','["A. Sodium (Na+)", "B. Chloride (Cl-)", "C. Potassium (K+)", "D. Calcium (Ca2+)"]','C','The resting membrane potential of ventricular myocytes (about -90 mV) is mainly determined by potassium equilibrium. At rest, K+ conductance is high (via inwardly rectifying K+ channels, IK1), and K+ diffuses down its concentration gradient out of the cell, leaving negative charges behind and creating the negative resting potential.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','easy','mcq','Cardiac muscle has an absolute refractory period that lasts almost as long as the contraction itself. What is the primary physiological importance of this?','["A. It allows the heart to generate higher force", "B. It speeds up ventricular conduction", "C. It increases cardiac output during exercise", "D. It prevents tetanic (sustained) contraction of the heart"]','D','The absolute refractory period of cardiac muscle lasts approximately 200-250 ms, nearly as long as the contraction (~300 ms). This prevents summation and tetanus, which would be fatal because the heart must relax between contractions to fill with blood. In skeletal muscle, the action potential is only 1-2 ms and contractions can summate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','medium','mcq','Which of the following best describes the role of the T-tubule system in cardiac muscle contraction?','["A. To transmit the action potential rapidly into the interior of the cell, triggering calcium release", "B. To provide the main path for action potential conduction between cells", "C. To synthesise ATP for muscle contraction", "D. To remove calcium from the cytoplasm after contraction"]','A','T-tubules (transverse tubules) invaginate the sarcolemma and carry the action potential deep into the cardiac myocyte. This ensures that L-type calcium channels in the T-tubule membrane are activated simultaneously throughout the cell. The calcium influx triggers massive calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum (CICR), allowing synchronous contraction of the entire myocyte.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','hard','mcq','A pharmacologist studies a drug that specifically blocks L-type calcium channels in the heart. Which phase of the ventricular action potential would be most directly affected?','["A. Phase 0 (rapid depolarisation)", "B. Phase 2 (plateau)", "C. Phase 1 (initial repolarisation)", "D. Phase 4 (resting)"]','B','L-type (dihydropyridine) calcium channels open during phase 2 (the plateau) of the ventricular action potential. Blocking these channels (e.g., with verapamil, diltiazem) shortens the plateau, reduces calcium entry, and decreases contractile force. Phase 0 is driven by fast Na+ channels; Phase 1 by transient outward K+ current; Phase 4 involves If (funny current) in pacemaker cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','hard','mcq','The cardiac muscle is described as a "functional syncytium." Action potentials spread from cell to cell through which structure?','["A. Voltage-gated sodium channels on the sarcolemma", "B. Motor nerve fibres innervating each myocyte", "C. Gap junctions (nexuses) within intercalated discs", "D. Calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"]','C','Gap junctions (connexons, primarily connexin-43) form low-resistance electrical connections between adjacent cardiac myocytes at the intercalated discs. This allows the action potential to spread from cell to cell without requiring individual neural innervation of every myocyte. The atria form one functional syncytium and the ventricles another, separated by the fibrous AV ring.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','easy','mcq','What protein, when bound to calcium, allows the myosin cross-bridge to attach to actin during cardiac muscle contraction?','["A. Tropomyosin", "B. Myosin ATPase", "C. Titin", "D. Troponin C"]','D','Troponin C (TnC) is the calcium-sensing component of the troponin complex. At rest, tropomyosin blocks the myosin-binding sites on actin. When calcium binds to TnC, a conformational change shifts tropomyosin, exposing binding sites and allowing myosin cross-bridges to attach to actin and generate force. Troponin I and T are the inhibitory and tropomyosin-binding subunits respectively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','medium','mcq','In the SA node action potential, unlike ventricular muscle, phase 0 (depolarisation) is primarily carried by:','["A. L-type Ca2+ channels (ICa,L)", "B. Fast Na+ channels (INa)", "C. Transient outward K+ current (Ito)", "D. Hyperpolarisation-activated If channels"]','A','SA node cells have a depolarised resting potential (approximately -60 mV), at which fast Na+ channels are inactivated. Phase 0 depolarisation in the SA node is therefore carried by L-type Ca2+ channels. This produces a slower, more graded upstroke compared with ventricular cells. This difference is exploited clinically - Ca2+ channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) slow SA node rate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Muscle Structure & Action Potential','medium','mcq','Which of the following is a TRUE comparison between cardiac and skeletal muscle?','["A. Cardiac muscle requires no extracellular calcium for contraction", "B. Cardiac muscle has a much longer action potential duration than skeletal muscle", "C. Cardiac muscle can undergo tetanus because of its prolonged action potential", "D. Skeletal muscle cells are connected by intercalated discs"]','B','The ventricular action potential lasts approximately 250-300 ms, whereas the skeletal muscle action potential lasts only 1-2 ms. The long cardiac action potential creates a long refractory period preventing tetanus. Cardiac muscle DOES require extracellular calcium (via L-type channels), and intercalated discs are unique to cardiac muscle.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','easy','mcq','During which phase of the cardiac cycle are the aortic and pulmonary valves OPEN?','["A. Isovolumetric relaxation", "B. Ventricular filling", "C. Ventricular ejection (systole)", "D. Isovolumetric contraction"]','C','The semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonary) open when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic/pulmonary artery pressure at the onset of ejection, and close when ventricular pressure falls below these at end of ejection. During isovolumetric contraction and relaxation all four valves are closed. During filling, only the AV valves (mitral and tricuspid) are open.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','medium','mcq','At the beginning of isovolumetric contraction, which of the following best describes the state of the cardiac valves?','["A. Mitral valve open, aortic valve closed", "B. Aortic valve open, mitral valve closed", "C. All valves are open", "D. All valves are closed"]','D','Isovolumetric contraction begins when electrical systole triggers ventricular muscle contraction. Ventricular pressure rises above atrial pressure, closing the mitral (and tricuspid) valve. However, ventricular pressure has not yet exceeded aortic pressure, so the aortic valve remains closed. Volume is constant (isovolumetric) because all valves are shut. This phase ends when LV pressure exceeds approximately 80 mmHg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','medium','mcq','In the Wiggers diagram, the dicrotic notch on the aortic pressure waveform corresponds to which event?','["A. Closure of the aortic valve", "B. Closure of the mitral valve", "C. Opening of the aortic valve", "D. Peak ventricular systolic pressure"]','A','The dicrotic notch (incisura) on the aortic pressure trace is produced by a brief backflow of blood that causes the aortic valve leaflets to snap shut. This marks the end of ventricular ejection and the beginning of isovolumetric relaxation. It is followed by a slight pressure rebound due to elastic recoil of the aorta (Windkessel effect).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','easy','mcq','The first heart sound (S1) is produced by:','["A. Closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves", "B. Closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves", "C. Opening of the mitral and tricuspid valves", "D. Turbulent blood flow through the aortic valve"]','B','S1 ("lub") occurs at the start of systole and is produced by the abrupt closure of the AV valves (mitral and tricuspid), which causes vibrations in the blood, valves, and surrounding structures. S2 ("dub") is produced by closure of the semilunar valves at the end of systole. S3 and S4 are extra diastolic sounds.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','medium','mcq','A 60-year-old man has an end-diastolic volume (EDV) of 120 mL and an end-systolic volume (ESV) of 50 mL. What is his ejection fraction (EF)?','["A. Approximately 42%", "B. Approximately 70%", "C. Approximately 58%", "D. Approximately 25%"]','C','Ejection fraction = (EDV - ESV) / EDV x 100 = (120 - 50) / 120 x 100 = 58.3%. Normal EF is 55% or greater. In heart failure with reduced EF (HFrEF), EF is less than 40%. Stroke volume here is 70 mL (120 - 50), which is normal at rest.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','hard','mcq','Which of the following correctly describes the "a wave" of the jugular venous pulse (JVP)?','["A. It corresponds to right ventricular systole", "B. It reflects tricuspid valve closure", "C. It corresponds to venous filling during diastole", "D. It is produced by right atrial contraction at end-diastole"]','D','The "a" wave of the JVP represents right atrial contraction (at end-diastole). It is absent in atrial fibrillation. A prominent cannon a wave occurs in complete heart block when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve. The "c" wave follows (tricuspid bulging), then the "x" descent, "v" wave (venous filling), and "y" descent (tricuspid opening).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','hard','mcq','At a heart rate of 75 bpm, the duration of one cardiac cycle is approximately 0.8 seconds. What proportion of this cycle is occupied by diastole?','["A. Approximately two-thirds", "B. Approximately one-third", "C. Approximately one-half", "D. Approximately three-quarters"]','A','At rest (75 bpm, cycle = 0.8 s), systole occupies approximately 0.27 s and diastole approximately 0.53 s, which is approximately two-thirds of the cycle. As heart rate increases, diastole shortens disproportionately more than systole. At very high heart rates in tachyarrhythmias, diastolic filling time is critically shortened, reducing stroke volume and cardiac output.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','medium','mcq','During rapid ventricular filling in early diastole, what drives blood from the atria into the ventricles?','["A. Active atrial contraction", "B. Negative ventricular pressure relative to atrial pressure following ventricular relaxation", "C. Increased pulmonary vein pressure", "D. Closure of the semilunar valves"]','B','During isovolumetric relaxation, ventricular pressure drops rapidly. When LV pressure falls below left atrial pressure (~5 mmHg), the mitral valve opens and blood rushes in passively. This rapid early diastolic filling accounts for approximately 70-80% of stroke volume at rest. Active atrial contraction (atrial kick) contributes the remaining 20-30% and is particularly important at high heart rates.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','easy','mcq','Normal left ventricular end-diastolic pressure (LVEDP) is approximately:','["A. 0-2 mmHg", "B. 20-25 mmHg", "C. 5-12 mmHg", "D. 30-35 mmHg"]','C','Normal LVEDP is 5-12 mmHg. This reflects the filling pressure of the left ventricle at end-diastole. It is elevated (>18 mmHg) in left heart failure and is clinically assessed indirectly by pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) via a Swan-Ganz catheter. LVEDP is a key determinant of preload.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','The Cardiac Cycle','medium','mcq','On a left ventricular pressure-volume (PV) loop, the width of the loop represents:','["A. Heart rate", "B. Cardiac output", "C. End-diastolic pressure", "D. Stroke volume"]','D','On a PV loop, the x-axis is volume and the y-axis is pressure. The loop is traced counterclockwise: filling (bottom right), isovolumetric contraction (right side), ejection (top), isovolumetric relaxation (left side). The horizontal width of the loop (EDV minus ESV) equals the stroke volume. A leftward shift of the ESV point indicates increased contractility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','easy','mcq','Cardiac output is calculated as:','["A. Heart rate x Stroke volume", "B. Stroke volume x End-diastolic volume", "C. Heart rate x Blood pressure", "D. Stroke volume / Heart rate"]','A','Cardiac output (CO) = Heart Rate (HR) x Stroke Volume (SV). Normal resting CO is approximately 5 L/min (HR = 70 bpm x SV = 70 mL). CO can increase to 20-25 L/min during vigorous exercise in trained athletes. The three primary determinants of SV are preload, afterload, and myocardial contractility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','medium','mcq','According to the Frank-Starling law, increasing ventricular preload results in:','["A. Decreased stroke volume due to overstretching of sarcomeres", "B. Increased stroke volume up to an optimal filling point", "C. No change in stroke volume - stroke volume is independent of preload", "D. Decreased contractility of the myocardium"]','B','The Frank-Starling mechanism states that the heart pumps all the blood that returns to it. As ventricular filling (preload) increases, sarcomere length increases toward its optimum (~2.2 micrometres), increasing the overlap between actin and myosin filaments and the sensitivity of troponin C to calcium. This results in greater force of contraction and a larger stroke volume.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','medium','mcq','A 45-year-old man receives an intravenous fluid bolus during surgery. His cardiac output increases. Which of the following best explains this according to Starling''''s law?','["A. Increased afterload stimulates the myocardium directly", "B. The IV fluid directly stimulates sympathetic receptors in the heart", "C. Increased venous return raises preload, stretching myocytes and increasing stroke volume", "D. Fluid loading decreases heart rate, which increases filling time"]','C','Intravenous fluid increases circulating blood volume, increases venous return, raises right atrial pressure, increases ventricular end-diastolic volume (EDV/preload), stretches myocytes, increases force via Frank-Starling mechanism, producing a larger stroke volume and greater cardiac output. This is the physiological basis for fluid resuscitation in hypovolaemia and for optimising preload perioperatively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','easy','mcq','The cardiac index is defined as:','["A. Stroke volume divided by body surface area", "B. Heart rate multiplied by body weight", "C. Mean arterial pressure divided by cardiac output", "D. Cardiac output divided by body surface area"]','D','The cardiac index (CI) normalises cardiac output for body size: CI = CO / BSA (body surface area). Normal CI is 2.5-4.0 L/min/m2. It is used to compare cardiac function across patients of different sizes. A CI less than 2.2 L/min/m2 is a criterion for cardiogenic shock.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','medium','mcq','Which of the following would cause an upward shift of the Frank-Starling curve, indicating increased contractility?','["A. Sympathetic stimulation and catecholamines", "B. Vagal stimulation", "C. Myocardial infarction", "D. Calcium channel blockade"]','A','Positive inotropic agents (sympathetic stimulation, catecholamines, cardiac glycosides, increased heart rate via Bowditch effect) shift the Starling curve upward and to the left - at any given preload (EDV), the stroke volume and cardiac output are higher. Conversely, negative inotropes (beta-blockers, Ca2+ channel blockers, MI) shift the curve downward/right.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','hard','mcq','Using the Fick principle, arteriovenous oxygen difference is 5 mL O2 per 100 mL blood and oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min. What is the cardiac output?','["A. 2.5 L/min", "B. 5.0 L/min", "C. 7.5 L/min", "D. 10.0 L/min"]','B','Fick principle: CO = VO2 / (CaO2 - CvO2). CO = 250 mL O2/min divided by (5 mL O2/100 mL x 10 dL/L) = 250 / 50 = 5.0 L/min. This is the normal resting cardiac output. The Fick method uses conservation of mass: oxygen consumed by the tissues equals the oxygen delivered minus the oxygen returning.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','medium','mcq','Which of the following is the PRIMARY physiological determinant of cardiac output during moderate exercise?','["A. Increased afterload due to rising blood pressure", "B. Decreased peripheral resistance causing cardiac rebound", "C. Increased venous return and sympathetic stimulation increasing both HR and SV", "D. Parasympathetic withdrawal alone"]','C','During exercise, CO can increase 4- to 7-fold. The primary drivers are: (1) increased venous return (muscle pump, venoconstriction) that raises preload via Frank-Starling effect; and (2) sympathetic activation that increases HR (chronotropy) and contractility (inotropy). Parasympathetic withdrawal also contributes to tachycardia early in exercise.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','hard','mcq','A patient in cardiogenic shock has cardiac output of 2.0 L/min, blood pressure 80/50 mmHg (MAP approximately 60 mmHg) and central venous pressure of 20 mmHg. What does the high CVP with low CO indicate?','["A. Hypovolaemia with peripheral vasodilatation", "B. Normal cardiac function with low systemic vascular resistance", "C. Excessive parasympathetic tone causing reduced preload", "D. A hypoeffective heart unable to pump adequate output despite elevated filling pressure"]','D','In cardiogenic shock, the failing ventricle cannot generate adequate forward flow (low CO, low BP) despite elevated filling pressures (high CVP/PCWP). This is the classic forward failure and backward failure combination. The high CVP reflects damming of blood behind the failing right (or left) ventricle. Treatment focuses on inotropic support and afterload reduction rather than further fluid loading.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','easy','mcq','Afterload in the left ventricle is best approximated clinically by:','["A. Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)", "B. Left ventricular end-diastolic pressure", "C. Pulmonary artery pressure", "D. Central venous pressure"]','A','Afterload is the load against which the ventricle must contract during ejection. For the left ventricle, it is primarily determined by systemic vascular resistance (SVR) and aortic impedance. Clinically, SVR = (MAP - CVP) / CO x 80. Elevated afterload (e.g., hypertension, aortic stenosis) reduces stroke volume; vasodilators (e.g., ACE inhibitors, nitroprusside) reduce afterload and improve CO in heart failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Output & Starling''s Law','medium','mcq','The Bowditch (Treppe) effect refers to:','["A. Decreased contractility with increasing heart rate", "B. Increased contractility with increasing heart rate due to greater intracellular calcium accumulation", "C. The Frank-Starling relationship between EDV and stroke volume", "D. Negative feedback regulation of cardiac output by baroreceptors"]','B','The Bowditch (treppe or staircase) effect describes the positive force-frequency relationship in the heart: as heart rate increases, each subsequent contraction is stronger. This occurs because more frequent action potentials mean more calcium influx per unit time with less time for the SR to fully resequester calcium, resulting in progressively higher intracellular Ca2+ and greater contractile force. This is distinct from the Frank-Starling mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','easy','mcq','The sinoatrial (SA) node normally determines heart rate. What is the intrinsic rate of the SA node?','["A. 20-40 bpm", "B. 40-60 bpm", "C. 60-100 bpm", "D. 100-150 bpm"]','C','The intrinsic firing rate of the SA node is 60-100 bpm, making it the dominant pacemaker of the heart (highest automaticity). The AV node has an intrinsic rate of 40-60 bpm, and ventricular Purkinje fibres fire at 20-40 bpm. Under sympathetic dominance (e.g., exercise), SA node rate increases; under parasympathetic (vagal) dominance (e.g., sleep), rate decreases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','medium','mcq','Acetylcholine released by vagal (parasympathetic) fibres slows heart rate by acting on which receptor and ion channel?','["A. Beta-1 receptors increasing intracellular cAMP", "B. Alpha-1 receptors causing vasoconstriction", "C. Nicotinic receptors on the SA node cells", "D. Muscarinic M2 receptors opening IKACh channels, hyperpolarising the SA node"]','D','Vagal release of acetylcholine acts on muscarinic M2 receptors on SA node cells, activating Gi protein, which opens acetylcholine-sensitive K+ channels (IKACh). The resultant K+ efflux hyperpolarises the cell and reduces the slope of phase 4 (slow depolarisation), slowing spontaneous firing. Atropine blocks M2 receptors and therefore accelerates heart rate (useful in bradycardia).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','medium','mcq','Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate primarily by acting on which receptor type and what is the intracellular mechanism?','["A. Beta-1 receptors increasing cAMP via Gs protein, enhancing If and ICa,L", "B. Alpha-2 receptors decreasing cAMP", "C. Muscarinic M2 receptors increasing K+ conductance", "D. Beta-2 receptors increasing cGMP"]','A','Norepinephrine (and epinephrine) act on beta-1 adrenoceptors (Gs-coupled) in the SA node, increasing cAMP via adenylyl cyclase. This increases If (funny current - the pacemaker current) and ICa,L, steepening the slope of phase 4 depolarisation, leading to faster spontaneous firing (positive chronotropy). Beta-blockers (atenolol, metoprolol) antagonise this effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','hard','mcq','A 25-year-old sprinter''s heart rate rises from 65 to 180 bpm during a sprint. The initial rapid rise in heart rate (first 5-10 seconds) is primarily due to:','["A. Increased sympathetic outflow to the SA node", "B. Withdrawal of parasympathetic (vagal) tone", "C. Release of circulating epinephrine from the adrenal medulla", "D. Activation of arterial baroreceptors"]','B','The initial rapid increase in heart rate at the onset of exercise is primarily due to withdrawal of parasympathetic (vagal) tone, which acts within seconds. Sympathetic activation is slower and accounts for heart rates above approximately 100 bpm. Circulating catecholamines from the adrenal medulla contribute but are even slower (seconds to minutes).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','easy','mcq','Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and aortic arch detect changes in blood pressure. An acute rise in blood pressure would cause which reflex response?','["A. Increased heart rate and vasoconstriction", "B. Increased heart rate and vasodilatation", "C. Decreased heart rate and vasodilatation", "D. No change in heart rate"]','C','Baroreceptors (stretch receptors) increase firing when blood pressure rises. This activates the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), increasing vagal outflow to the heart (slowing HR) and decreasing sympathetic outflow (causing vasodilatation). This is the baroreflex (Hering reflex), which acts as a negative-feedback buffer preventing acute BP swings. It is why carotid sinus massage can terminate SVT.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','medium','mcq','The Bainbridge reflex refers to:','["A. Slowing of heart rate in response to raised arterial pressure", "B. Reflex bradycardia induced by carotid sinus massage", "C. Vasovagal syncope triggered by standing", "D. Increase in heart rate caused by increased right atrial pressure/venous return"]','D','The Bainbridge reflex: increased venous return (raised right atrial pressure) stimulates atrial stretch receptors, which send signals via vagal afferents to the cardiovascular centre, resulting in an increase in heart rate (positive chronotropy). This helps the heart match its output to the increased venous return, complementing the Frank-Starling mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','hard','mcq','A drug specifically increases the slope of the pacemaker potential (phase 4) in the SA node. What effect would this drug have?','["A. Tachycardia", "B. Bradycardia", "C. No change in heart rate", "D. Prolonged PR interval"]','A','The SA node exhibits spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation (pacemaker potential) driven mainly by the funny current (If). Any drug or intervention that steepens the slope of phase 4 causes threshold to be reached sooner, increasing the firing rate and producing tachycardia. Conversely, If inhibitors (e.g., ivabradine) flatten the slope, reducing heart rate without affecting contractility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','medium','mcq','In a healthy athlete with high vagal tone, which pattern would be expected?','["A. Low HRV with low resting heart rate", "B. High HRV with low resting heart rate", "C. Low HRV with high resting heart rate", "D. No relationship between HRV and vagal tone"]','B','Well-trained athletes have higher resting vagal tone, leading to sinus bradycardia (resting HR 40-55 bpm) and high heart rate variability (HRV). High HRV is a marker of cardiovascular health and autonomic flexibility. Low HRV is associated with poor outcomes in heart failure and post-MI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','easy','mcq','Which drug class is used clinically to slow heart rate by blocking beta-1 adrenoceptors in the SA node?','["A. Calcium channel blockers (dihydropyridines)", "B. ACE inhibitors", "C. Beta-adrenoceptor antagonists (beta-blockers)", "D. Loop diuretics"]','C','Beta-1 adrenoceptor antagonists (e.g., metoprolol, atenolol, bisoprolol, carvedilol) reduce sympathetic chronotropic stimulation of the SA node, slowing heart rate. They are used in rate control for atrial fibrillation, angina, post-MI, and heart failure. Dihydropyridine CCBs (e.g., amlodipine) do NOT slow HR; non-dihydropyridines (verapamil, diltiazem) do.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Heart Rate Regulation','medium','mcq','The cardiovascular centre in the brainstem that regulates heart rate and vascular tone is located in the:','["A. Thalamus", "B. Cerebral cortex", "C. Cerebellum", "D. Medulla oblongata"]','D','The cardiovascular centre (comprising the vasomotor centre and cardiac centre) is located in the medulla oblongata (specifically in the reticular formation, including the nucleus tractus solitarius, nucleus ambiguus, and ventrolateral medulla). It integrates inputs from baroreceptors, chemoreceptors, and higher brain centres and sends outputs via sympathetic and parasympathetic pathways.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','easy','mcq','Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is most accurately calculated as:','["A. Diastolic pressure + 1/3 (Systolic - Diastolic)", "B. (Systolic + Diastolic) / 2", "C. Systolic pressure - Diastolic pressure", "D. Cardiac output x Heart rate"]','A','MAP = DBP + 1/3 (PP), where PP (pulse pressure) = SBP - DBP. At a typical BP of 120/80 mmHg: MAP = 80 + 1/3(40) = 80 + 13.3 = approximately 93 mmHg. The 1/3 factor accounts for diastole occupying approximately 2/3 of the cardiac cycle. MAP represents the average perfusion pressure driving blood through the tissues.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','medium','mcq','A patient has a blood pressure of 150/90 mmHg. What is their mean arterial pressure (MAP)?','["A. 120 mmHg", "B. 110 mmHg", "C. 100 mmHg", "D. 130 mmHg"]','B','MAP = DBP + 1/3 x PP = 90 + 1/3 x (150 - 90) = 90 + 1/3 x 60 = 90 + 20 = 110 mmHg. A MAP above 100 mmHg is consistent with hypertension. MAP is the key pressure driving organ perfusion; a MAP of at least 65 mmHg is the target in septic shock resuscitation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','medium','mcq','Pulse pressure is increased in which of the following conditions?','["A. Haemorrhagic shock", "B. Cardiac tamponade", "C. Aortic regurgitation", "D. Severe aortic stenosis"]','C','Pulse pressure = SBP - DBP. It increases when stroke volume is large (high SBP) AND/OR diastolic run-off is high (low DBP). Aortic regurgitation causes both: high SBP (large stroke volume due to regurgitant volume) and low DBP (rapid diastolic run-off back through the incompetent valve). Other causes: patent ductus arteriosus, arteriovenous fistula, thyrotoxicosis. Tamponade and haemorrhage narrow pulse pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','easy','mcq','Blood pressure (MAP) is determined by MAP = CO x SVR. If cardiac output doubles while SVR halves, what happens to MAP?','["A. MAP doubles", "B. MAP quadruples", "C. MAP falls by half", "D. MAP remains unchanged"]','D','MAP = CO x SVR. If CO doubles (x2) and SVR halves (x0.5): new MAP = (2 x CO) x (0.5 x SVR) = 1.0 x CO x SVR = unchanged. This is why patients with high-output states (anaemia, AV fistula) can maintain near-normal BP despite low SVR - the CO compensates. Conversely, in septic shock, massive SVR fall causes MAP to drop despite elevated CO.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','hard','mcq','The Windkessel effect of the aorta refers to:','["A. Elastic recoil of the aorta during diastole maintaining continuous blood flow", "B. The increase in MAP during systole", "C. The ability of veins to act as blood reservoirs", "D. Arteriolar vasoconstriction increasing diastolic pressure"]','A','During systole, the aorta and large elastic arteries distend to store some ejected blood. During diastole, elastic recoil of the aorta propels this stored blood forward, maintaining diastolic pressure and continuous non-pulsatile capillary flow. With age or atherosclerosis, the aorta stiffens - stroke volume is ejected into a stiffer tube, raising SBP and lowering DBP (wide pulse pressure, isolated systolic hypertension).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','medium','mcq','Korotkoff sounds used in auscultatory blood pressure measurement are produced by:','["A. Closure of the mitral valve", "B. Turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery as cuff pressure is released", "C. Resonance of the stethoscope diaphragm", "D. Carotid pulse transmitted to the arm"]','B','When the sphygmomanometer cuff is inflated above systolic pressure, flow stops. As cuff pressure is gradually released, turbulent flow through the partially occluded brachial artery creates audible Korotkoff sounds. Phase I (first tapping sound) = systolic pressure. Sounds muffle then disappear (Phase V) = diastolic pressure. Below diastolic pressure, flow becomes laminar again (silent).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','hard','mcq','Autoregulation of blood flow in the brain maintains constant cerebral blood flow over which MAP range?','["A. 30-60 mmHg", "B. 100-180 mmHg", "C. 60-150 mmHg", "D. 50-80 mmHg"]','C','Cerebral autoregulation maintains constant blood flow over a MAP range of approximately 60-150 mmHg through myogenic and metabolic mechanisms. Below 60 mmHg (vasodilation maxed out), cerebral ischaemia occurs. Above 150 mmHg (vasoconstriction maxed out), forced distension and hypertensive encephalopathy occur. In chronic hypertension, the autoregulatory curve shifts rightward.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','medium','mcq','Which of the following is the PRIMARY long-term regulator of blood pressure?','["A. Arterial baroreceptors", "B. Chemoreceptors in the carotid body", "C. The Bainbridge reflex", "D. Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and renal fluid regulation"]','D','While baroreceptors and chemoreceptors regulate blood pressure on a second-to-minute timescale, the primary LONG-TERM regulator is the kidney through the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and pressure natriuresis. The kidney sets the long-term blood pressure level by adjusting fluid volume. Baroreceptors reset to any sustained new level within 1-3 days.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','easy','mcq','According to Poiseuille''s law, resistance to blood flow is MOST sensitive to changes in which variable?','["A. Vessel radius", "B. Blood viscosity", "C. Vessel length", "D. Cardiac output"]','A','Poiseuille''s law: R = 8nL / pr4. Resistance is inversely proportional to the FOURTH power of radius. Therefore, halving vessel radius increases resistance by 16-fold. This is why arterioles (which can vary their radius through smooth muscle contraction) are the primary site of resistance regulation in the circulation. Small changes in arteriolar radius have enormous effects on blood flow.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Arterial Blood Pressure & Mean Arterial Pressure','medium','mcq','A patient develops isolated systolic hypertension (SBP >160 mmHg, DBP normal). This is most commonly due to:','["A. Increased cardiac output in young athletes", "B. Increased arterial stiffness with age reducing the Windkessel effect", "C. Renal artery stenosis", "D. Primary hyperaldosteronism"]','B','Isolated systolic hypertension is the most common form of hypertension in elderly patients. With ageing, the aorta and large arteries become stiffer (reduced elastin, increased collagen, calcium deposition). The stiff aorta cannot expand adequately during systole, resulting in higher systolic peaks; it also cannot sustain diastolic pressure as well, so DBP may fall or stay normal, widening pulse pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','easy','mcq','The Starling forces governing fluid movement across the capillary wall are:','["A. Arterial pressure and venous pressure only", "B. Osmotic pressure and lymphatic drainage only", "C. Capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, and interstitial oncotic pressure", "D. Blood viscosity and capillary radius only"]','C','Starling''s equation: Net filtration = Kf [(Pc - Pi) - s(pc - pi)], where Pc = capillary hydrostatic pressure, Pi = interstitial hydrostatic pressure, pc = plasma oncotic pressure (mainly albumin, ~28 mmHg), pi = interstitial oncotic pressure. Net filtration occurs at the arterial end and net reabsorption at venous end. Lymphatics drain excess filtrate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','medium','mcq','Capillary hydrostatic pressure at the arterial end of a systemic capillary is approximately:','["A. 5-10 mmHg", "B. 70-80 mmHg", "C. 20-25 mmHg", "D. 35-40 mmHg"]','D','Capillary hydrostatic pressure at the arterial end is approximately 30-40 mmHg (mean ~35-38 mmHg). This falls to approximately 15-20 mmHg at the venous end. This gradient, along with plasma oncotic pressure (~28 mmHg), determines the pattern of filtration at the arterial end and reabsorption at the venous end.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','medium','mcq','A patient with severe hypoalbuminaemia develops pitting oedema. Which Starling force is most directly disrupted?','["A. Reduced plasma oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure", "B. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure", "C. Increased interstitial oncotic pressure", "D. Decreased lymphatic drainage"]','A','Plasma oncotic pressure (~28 mmHg) is the primary force retaining fluid within the capillary. It is generated mainly by albumin. In hypoalbuminaemia (nephrotic syndrome, liver failure, malnutrition), plasma oncotic pressure falls, reducing reabsorption at the venous end, leaving more fluid in the interstitium. Classic causes include nephrotic syndrome, liver cirrhosis, and kwashiorkor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','easy','mcq','The primary mechanism by which oxygen and nutrients cross the capillary wall to reach tissue cells is:','["A. Active transport via membrane pumps", "B. Passive diffusion down concentration gradients", "C. Pinocytosis (vesicular transcytosis)", "D. Bulk flow through lymphatic channels"]','B','The dominant mechanism of exchange at the capillary level is diffusion - passive movement of solutes down their concentration gradients. Lipid-soluble substances (O2, CO2, most drugs) diffuse freely through the entire endothelial membrane. Water-soluble substances diffuse through intercellular clefts (pores ~6-7 nm). The enormous surface area of capillaries and short diffusion distances make diffusion highly efficient.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','hard','mcq','Vasomotion in the microcirculation refers to:','["A. Smooth muscle contraction causing arteriolar vasoconstriction in hypertension", "B. Rhythmic contraction of venules to aid lymphatic drainage", "C. Periodic intermittent opening and closing of precapillary sphincters regulating capillary blood flow", "D. Oscillation of blood pressure due to the baroreceptor reflex"]','C','Vasomotion is the periodic (every few seconds to minutes) opening and closing of precapillary sphincters and metarterioles. This creates intermittent blood flow through individual capillaries. It is regulated locally by tissue oxygen tension - when metabolic demand is high and O2 falls, sphincters relax and more capillaries open. This increases the functional capillary surface area and reduces diffusion distances to cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','medium','mcq','The lymphatic system returns fluid to the circulation because lymphatic capillaries:','["A. Are closed vessels with no connection to veins", "B. Pump fluid actively using smooth muscle only", "C. Filter lymph through lymph nodes before reabsorbing it into arteries", "D. Have one-way valves and ultimately drain into the subclavian veins"]','D','Lymphatic capillaries are permeable blind-ended tubes that collect interstitial fluid and proteins. They have one-way flap valves (anchoring filaments allow them to open when interstitial pressure rises). Lymph flows via contracting lymphangions and skeletal muscle pump through lymph nodes, then collects into the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct, draining into the subclavian veins. Impaired lymphatic drainage causes lymphoedema.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','hard','mcq','In a patient with right heart failure and elevated CVP (15 mmHg), which Starling force is most directly responsible for the resulting systemic oedema?','["A. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure due to elevated venous back-pressure", "B. Decreased plasma oncotic pressure", "C. Decreased lymphatic drainage", "D. Increased interstitial oncotic pressure"]','A','In right heart failure, the inability to pump blood forward raises venous pressure. This elevated venous back-pressure is transmitted retrogradely to the capillaries, raising capillary hydrostatic pressure. When capillary hydrostatic pressure exceeds the opposing oncotic pressure, net filtration increases and fluid accumulates in the interstitium, causing systemic oedema, peripheral oedema, and ascites.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','medium','mcq','Fenestrated capillaries are found in which organs?','["A. Brain and skeletal muscle", "B. Kidneys (glomeruli), intestinal villi, and endocrine glands", "C. Liver sinusoids and spleen", "D. Cardiac muscle and lungs only"]','B','Fenestrated capillaries have pores (60-80 nm) through the endothelium, allowing rapid transcapillary exchange of water and solutes. They are found in organs requiring high filtration: glomeruli (GFR), intestinal villi (nutrient absorption), and endocrine glands (hormone secretion). Continuous capillaries (brain, muscle) are tightest. Sinusoidal capillaries (liver, spleen, bone marrow) are most permeable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','easy','mcq','Which structure is the primary site of resistance in the systemic circulation?','["A. Aorta", "B. Capillaries", "C. Arterioles", "D. Venules"]','C','Arterioles are the primary site of resistance in the systemic circulation (about 50% of total vascular resistance). They have thick smooth muscle walls that can dramatically change their calibre in response to local metabolic factors, neural input (sympathetic), and hormones (angiotensin II, noradrenaline). Because of the r4 dependence (Poiseuille''s law), small changes in arteriolar radius have large effects on resistance and blood flow.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Microcirculation & Capillary Exchange','medium','mcq','Reactive hyperaemia (increased blood flow following a period of occlusion) is primarily mediated by:','["A. Sympathetic stimulation opening collateral vessels", "B. Reflex from baroreceptors increasing local vessel diameter", "C. Increased cardiac output after release of occlusion", "D. Accumulation of local metabolic vasodilators (CO2, H+, adenosine, K+) during ischaemia"]','D','During ischaemia, oxygen is depleted and metabolites accumulate: CO2, H+ (acidosis), adenosine, K+, and prostaglandins. These cause local arteriolar dilation. When the occlusion is released, these vasodilators produce a surge in blood flow (reactive hyperaemia) that clears the accumulated metabolites. This is a classic demonstration of metabolic autoregulation of blood flow.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','easy','mcq','Central venous pressure (CVP) is the pressure in the:','["A. Superior vena cava or right atrium", "B. Brachial vein", "C. Pulmonary artery", "D. Left atrium"]','A','CVP is measured in the superior vena cava or right atrium (normal 0-8 mmHg, or 0-10 cmH2O). It reflects right atrial filling pressure and provides an estimate of right ventricular preload. It is elevated in right heart failure, cardiac tamponade, and fluid overload; it is low in hypovolaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','medium','mcq','Mean systemic filling pressure (Psf) reflects:','["A. The resistance of the arterioles to blood flow", "B. The average pressure throughout the systemic circuit, reflecting total blood volume and vascular compliance", "C. The diastolic pressure in the aorta", "D. Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure"]','B','Mean systemic filling pressure (approximately 7 mmHg in humans) is the pressure equilibrated throughout the systemic vasculature when the heart is stopped. It is determined by total blood volume and total vascular compliance. Increasing blood volume or decreasing compliance raises Psf, increasing the pressure gradient for venous return. The gradient driving venous return = Psf - right atrial pressure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','medium','mcq','The venous pump (skeletal muscle pump) facilitates venous return by:','["A. Generating negative pressure in large veins", "B. Stimulating smooth muscle in venous walls", "C. Compressing veins during muscle contraction and directing flow toward the heart via one-way valves", "D. Decreasing venous compliance to raise venous pressure"]','C','Skeletal muscles compress adjacent veins during contraction, propelling blood toward the heart. One-way venous valves prevent retrograde flow during muscle relaxation. The venous pump is especially important in the legs during walking/standing (preventing venous pooling and oedema) and during exercise (significantly enhancing venous return and CO). Incompetent valves lead to varicose veins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','hard','mcq','Approximately what percentage of total blood volume is contained in the venous system (veins and venules)?','["A. 10-15%", "B. 20-25%", "C. 80-90%", "D. 60-70%"]','D','The venous system (including venules and small veins) contains approximately 60-70% of total blood volume, making it the primary capacitance (reservoir) of the circulation. Arteries contain approximately 15%, capillaries 5%, and heart/lungs 20%. Sympathetic venoconstriction can rapidly mobilise this reservoir, increasing venous return and cardiac output - important in haemorrhage and exercise.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','medium','mcq','The respiratory pump enhances venous return because:','["A. Inspiration decreases intrathoracic pressure, expanding thoracic veins and right atrium, drawing blood in", "B. Expiration compresses the vena cava, increasing venous return", "C. Deep breathing increases cardiac rate via chemoreceptors", "D. Diaphragm contraction compresses the left ventricle"]','A','During inspiration, intrathoracic (and hence right atrial) pressure falls (becomes more negative, typically -4 to -8 mmHg). This expands the intrathoracic veins and right atrium, drawing blood in from peripheral veins (increased venous return). This is the respiratory pump and explains why venous return varies with respiration (visible in JVP).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','hard','mcq','On a cardiac output-venous return curve diagram (Guyton diagram), the intersection of the cardiac function curve and the venous return curve represents:','["A. Maximum cardiac output", "B. The steady-state operating point of the cardiovascular system", "C. Mean systemic filling pressure", "D. The point of cardiac failure"]','B','The Guyton diagram plots cardiac output and venous return curves together on the same axes (CO or VR vs. right atrial pressure). The two curves intersect at one point - the equilibrium (steady-state operating) point where cardiac output exactly equals venous return. Any perturbation (e.g., fluid loading, sympathetic stimulation) shifts one or both curves, moving the operating point to a new equilibrium.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','medium','mcq','A drug causes systemic venoconstriction. What is the direct effect on cardiac output?','["A. Decreased cardiac output due to increased afterload", "B. No change in cardiac output", "C. Increased cardiac output via increased venous return and preload", "D. Decreased cardiac output due to increased heart rate"]','C','Venoconstriction (e.g., due to sympathetic activation, vasopressin, noradrenaline) reduces venous capacitance, shifting blood from the venous reservoir to the central circulation. This increases mean systemic filling pressure (Psf), raising the pressure gradient for venous return. Increased venous return raises right atrial pressure, increasing RV preload, ultimately boosting cardiac output via Starling''s law.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','easy','mcq','Kussmaul''s sign (a paradoxical rise in JVP on inspiration) is seen in constrictive pericarditis because:','["A. Inspiration increases left ventricular output", "B. Inspiration causes pulmonary vasoconstriction", "C. Constrictive pericarditis reduces venous capacitance", "D. The non-distensible pericardium prevents the right heart from accommodating increased venous return on inspiration"]','D','Normally, inspiration increases venous return and RV filling, with the JVP falling. In constrictive pericarditis, the thickened fibrotic pericardium restricts overall cardiac volume. On inspiration, the increased venous return cannot be accommodated by the rigid RV, so blood backs up in the systemic veins and JVP rises paradoxically (Kussmaul''s sign). Also seen in cardiac tamponade and right ventricular infarction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','medium','mcq','Which of the following interventions would MOST effectively increase venous return in a haemorrhage patient?','["A. Raising the patient''s legs (Trendelenburg position) and IV fluid resuscitation", "B. Administration of a vasodilator", "C. Giving a beta-blocker to slow the heart", "D. Applying positive pressure ventilation"]','A','Raising the legs uses gravity to passively shift blood from the lower limb venous reservoir (~500 mL) to the central circulation, augmenting venous return and cardiac output. Simultaneously, IV fluid resuscitation increases total blood volume, raising mean systemic filling pressure. In haemorrhage, sympathetic venoconstriction also occurs reflexly. Positive pressure ventilation reduces venous return.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Venous Return & Venous Pressure','hard','mcq','Orthostatic hypotension occurs on standing because:','["A. Sympathetic tone decreases when standing", "B. Approximately 500-700 mL of blood pools in lower limb veins, reducing venous return and CO before the baroreflex compensates", "C. Standing causes a decrease in heart rate via vagal activation", "D. Blood pressure in the carotid sinus rises on standing"]','B','On standing, gravity causes approximately 500-700 mL to pool in the lower limb veins and splanchnic bed. This reduces venous return, stroke volume, and CO, causing a transient fall in BP. Normally, the baroreflex compensates within seconds (increased sympathetic outflow, increased HR, SVR, and venoconstriction). Failure of this reflex (autonomic neuropathy, hypovolaemia, drugs) causes symptomatic orthostatic hypotension.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','easy','mcq','The left anterior descending (LAD) artery supplies blood to which major territory of the heart?','["A. Right ventricle and inferior wall", "B. Left atrium and posterior left ventricle only", "C. Left ventricular anteroseptal wall, apex, and anterior left ventricle", "D. AV node and SA node predominantly"]','C','The LAD (left anterior descending) coronary artery, a branch of the left main coronary artery, supplies: the anterior wall of the left ventricle, interventricular septum (anterior 2/3), apex, and anterior papillary muscle. Occlusion causes anterior STEMI - the most common and most catastrophic MI territory. The LAD is often called the "widow maker" artery.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','medium','mcq','Coronary blood flow to the left ventricle is greatest during which phase of the cardiac cycle?','["A. Ventricular systole", "B. Isovolumetric contraction", "C. Atrial systole", "D. Early ventricular diastole"]','D','Left coronary artery flow is highest during early diastole, when the myocardium relaxes and intramyocardial compression is absent. During systole, myocardial contraction compresses intramural coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardial region, greatly reducing or even reversing left coronary flow. Right coronary flow is less affected because right ventricular systolic pressure is much lower.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','medium','mcq','The DOMINANT regulator of coronary blood flow during increased myocardial workload is:','["A. Local metabolic vasodilation (adenosine, CO2, K+, H+)", "B. Sympathetic vasoconstriction", "C. Angiotensin II", "D. Parasympathetic vasodilation"]','A','Coronary blood flow is primarily regulated by local myocardial metabolism. When O2 demand rises (increased HR, contractility), O2 tension falls, and metabolites accumulate: adenosine (most important), CO2, K+, H+, and prostaglandins. These are potent coronary vasodilators. Coronary blood flow can increase up to 5-fold during maximal exercise. Adenosine is used pharmacologically in cardiac stress testing.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','hard','mcq','Why is subendocardial ischaemia more common than subepicardial ischaemia during coronary artery disease?','["A. The subendocardium has a lower oxygen demand", "B. Subendocardial vessels face higher wall stress and compressive forces during systole, leaving less perfusion reserve", "C. Coronary arteries supply the subepicardium directly via epicardial branches", "D. The subendocardium has a higher density of sympathetic nerves"]','B','The subendocardium is the most vulnerable layer because: (1) it has the highest oxygen demand (greatest wall stress); (2) coronary vessels in the subendocardium are maximally compressed during systole (they rely almost entirely on diastolic flow); and (3) they are farthest from the epicardial coronary arteries. In coronary artery disease, diastolic perfusion pressure falls, and subendocardial ischaemia (ST depression on ECG) occurs first.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','easy','mcq','Normal coronary blood flow at rest is approximately:','["A. 50 mL/min", "B. 500 mL/min", "C. 225 mL/min", "D. 1000 mL/min"]','C','Normal resting coronary blood flow is approximately 200-250 mL/min, representing about 4-5% of total cardiac output. During maximal exercise, it can increase to 1000-1200 mL/min. The heart also has the highest oxygen extraction ratio of any organ at rest (~75%), so increased metabolic demand during exercise must be met predominantly by increasing blood flow rather than oxygen extraction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','hard','mcq','A patient with 90% stenosis of the right coronary artery develops symptoms at low workload. Which concept explains this?','["A. Increased sympathetic tone causing vasospasm", "B. High systemic vascular resistance reducing aortic pressure", "C. Reduced cardiac output from right heart overload", "D. Reduced coronary reserve - the stenosis limits maximal coronary vasodilation"]','D','Coronary reserve is the ability to increase blood flow above resting levels during increased demand. A tight stenosis (>70%) limits the maximum achievable coronary blood flow. At rest, autoregulatory vasodilation distal to the stenosis can maintain adequate flow. During exercise, the downstream vessels are already maximally dilated; any further demand cannot be met - ischaemia develops. This is assessed by fractional flow reserve (FFR).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','medium','mcq','In a right-dominant coronary circulation (most common), the posterior descending artery (PDA) originates from:','["A. Right coronary artery", "B. Left anterior descending artery", "C. Left circumflex artery", "D. Obtuse marginal artery"]','A','In approximately 70% of individuals, the right coronary artery (RCA) gives rise to the posterior descending artery (PDA) - this is "right dominance." The PDA supplies the posterior interventricular septum, posterior wall of both ventricles, and the AV node (in 90% of cases). In left-dominant circulation (10-15%), the PDA comes from the left circumflex.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','medium','mcq','Nitric oxide (NO) produced by coronary endothelium contributes to coronary vasodilation by:','["A. Directly opening L-type calcium channels", "B. Activating guanylyl cyclase to increase cGMP, relaxing smooth muscle", "C. Blocking beta-1 adrenoceptors", "D. Increasing prostaglandin synthesis"]','B','Nitric oxide (NO), produced by endothelial nitric oxide synthase (eNOS) from L-arginine, diffuses into vascular smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, raising cGMP. cGMP activates protein kinase G, which reduces intracellular calcium, causing smooth muscle relaxation and vasodilation. This is the mechanism of organic nitrates (GTN), which are NO donors used in angina. Sildenafil inhibits cGMP breakdown (PDE5 inhibitor).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','easy','mcq','The SA node is supplied by the SA nodal artery, which is a branch of the:','["A. Left anterior descending artery in all individuals", "B. Aorta directly", "C. Right coronary artery (in ~60%) or left circumflex (in ~40%)", "D. Right marginal artery"]','C','The SA nodal artery arises from the RCA in about 60% of individuals and from the left circumflex artery in the remaining approximately 40%. This is why occlusion of the proximal RCA (inferior MI) commonly causes sinus node dysfunction (sinus bradycardia, heart block). The AV node artery arises from the RCA in approximately 90% of individuals (right dominant circulation).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Coronary Circulation','medium','mcq','Angina pectoris occurs when:','["A. Myocardial oxygen supply exceeds demand", "B. Coronary arteries spasm in the presence of normal demand only", "C. The right ventricle becomes ischaemic only", "D. Coronary blood flow is insufficient for myocardial metabolic needs, causing ischaemia"]','D','Angina pectoris is chest pain caused by myocardial ischaemia when oxygen supply cannot meet myocardial oxygen demand. In stable angina, this typically occurs when a fixed coronary stenosis prevents adequate flow augmentation during exertion. In unstable angina, a disrupted plaque partially occludes the vessel. In Prinzmetal (variant) angina, coronary vasospasm temporarily reduces supply at rest.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','easy','mcq','The normal sequence of electrical conduction through the heart is:','["A. SA node to AV node to Bundle of His to Left and Right Bundle Branches to Purkinje fibres", "B. AV node to SA node to Bundle of His to Purkinje fibres", "C. SA node to Purkinje fibres to AV node to Bundle of His", "D. Purkinje fibres to SA node to AV node to Bundle Branches"]','A','The normal cardiac conduction pathway: SA node (right atrium, near SVC) to atrial myocardium to AV node (interatrial septum, near coronary sinus) to Bundle of His to left and right bundle branches to Purkinje fibres to ventricular myocardium. The AV node introduces a physiological delay (~0.1 s), allowing atrial systole to complete before ventricular activation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','medium','mcq','The physiological delay in conduction at the AV node is important because:','["A. It prevents the action potential from spreading to the Bundle of His", "B. It allows atrial contraction to complete before ventricular contraction begins", "C. It speeds up ventricular repolarisation", "D. It generates the P wave on the ECG"]','B','The AV node delay (~100 ms at rest) serves two key functions: (1) it allows atrial systole to complete, pumping the last 20-30% of ventricular filling (atrial kick) before the ventricles contract; and (2) it limits the rate at which atrial impulses can be transmitted to the ventricles, protecting against very rapid ventricular rates in atrial flutter/fibrillation. This delay is represented by the PR interval (normal 120-200 ms) on the ECG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','medium','mcq','Purkinje fibres are the fastest-conducting elements of the cardiac conduction system. Their conduction velocity is approximately:','["A. 0.02-0.05 m/s", "B. 0.3-0.5 m/s", "C. 1-4 m/s", "D. 10-15 m/s"]','C','Purkinje fibres conduct at 1-4 m/s, the fastest of any cardiac tissue. This rapid conduction ensures nearly simultaneous activation of the entire ventricular endocardium, allowing a synchronised, coordinated ventricular contraction. Compare: AV node approximately 0.05 m/s (slowest), atrial muscle approximately 1 m/s, ventricular muscle approximately 0.3-0.5 m/s.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','hard','mcq','A patient presents with complete heart block (third-degree AV block). If a junctional escape rhythm develops, what would the ventricular rate be?','["A. 60-100 bpm", "B. 100-150 bpm", "C. 20-40 bpm", "D. 40-60 bpm"]','D','In complete (third-degree) AV block, no impulses conduct from atria to ventricles. The AV junction (AV node/His bundle) can initiate an escape rhythm at its intrinsic rate of 40-60 bpm. If conduction fails further down, ventricular Purkinje fibres produce an idioventricular rhythm at 20-40 bpm (wide QRS). The P-P interval and R-R interval are completely independent (AV dissociation). Patients typically require pacemaker implantation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','easy','mcq','The PR interval on the ECG represents:','["A. Time for the impulse to travel from the SA node through the atria and AV node to the ventricles", "B. Time for ventricular repolarisation", "C. Duration of ventricular depolarisation", "D. Time for atrial repolarisation"]','A','The PR interval (normally 0.12-0.20 seconds) represents the time from onset of atrial depolarisation (P wave) to onset of ventricular depolarisation (QRS complex). It includes conduction through the atrial myocardium, AV node (main site of delay), Bundle of His, and bundle branches. Prolonged PR (>0.20 s) = first-degree heart block. Short PR (<0.12 s) = pre-excitation (e.g., WPW syndrome).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','hard','mcq','Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome is caused by:','["A. Fibrosis of the AV node causing heart block", "B. An accessory conduction pathway (Bundle of Kent) bypassing the AV node, causing pre-excitation", "C. SA node dysfunction causing bradycardia", "D. Complete block in the right bundle branch"]','B','WPW is caused by an accessory atrioventricular pathway (Bundle of Kent) that conducts faster than the AV node. This causes pre-excitation - the ventricle is activated early, producing a short PR interval, slurred upstroke (delta wave), and wide QRS on ECG. The accessory pathway creates a re-entry circuit that can cause paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (SVT). Treatment includes radiofrequency catheter ablation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','medium','mcq','Left bundle branch block (LBBB) causes which characteristic ECG finding?','["A. Narrow QRS complex with normal PR interval", "B. Short PR interval with delta waves", "C. Broad QRS (>120 ms) with absence of normal septal Q waves in left precordial leads", "D. Prolonged QT interval"]','C','LBBB produces a broad QRS complex (>120 ms) because left ventricular activation must occur via slow cell-to-cell spread rather than rapid Purkinje conduction. Characteristic ECG features: broad notched (M-shaped) R waves in I, aVL, V5-V6; broad S waves in V1; absence of normal septal Q waves (septal depolarisation is reversed). New LBBB with chest pain is treated as STEMI equivalent.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','medium','mcq','The "If" (funny) current in pacemaker cells is:','["A. An outward potassium current causing hyperpolarisation", "B. An inward calcium current sustaining the plateau", "C. A transient outward potassium current causing phase 1", "D. An inward mixed Na+/K+ current activated by hyperpolarisation, driving pacemaker potential"]','D','The If (or Ih, "funny" or "pacemaker") current is a hyperpolarisation-activated, cyclic nucleotide-gated (HCN) current that carries both Na+ and K+ inward. It is activated at voltages more negative than approximately -40 to -60 mV. It drives the slow depolarisation of phase 4 in SA node and other pacemaker cells. Ivabradine specifically blocks If channels, slowing HR without affecting contractility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','easy','mcq','Which ion channel is responsible for the rapid upstroke (phase 0) of the ventricular action potential?','["A. Voltage-gated sodium channels (INa)", "B. L-type calcium channels", "C. IK1 inward rectifier potassium channels", "D. If funny channels"]','A','Phase 0 (rapid depolarisation) of the ventricular action potential is generated by voltage-gated fast Na+ channels (INa). When the threshold potential (~-70 mV) is reached, Na+ channels rapidly open, allowing a massive Na+ influx that rapidly depolarises the cell to approximately +30 mV. Class I antiarrhythmics (e.g., lignocaine, flecainide) block these channels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Cardiac Conduction System','hard','mcq','The QT interval on the ECG represents the duration of which event?','["A. Atrial depolarisation and repolarisation", "B. Ventricular depolarisation (QRS) plus ventricular repolarisation (T wave)", "C. Conduction through the AV node and bundle branches only", "D. Atrial systole"]','B','The QT interval (from beginning of QRS to end of T wave) represents the total duration of ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation (ventricular electrical systole). Normal corrected QTc is <440 ms (men) or <460 ms (women). Prolonged QT predisposes to torsades de pointes (a potentially fatal ventricular arrhythmia). Causes: drugs (antiarrhythmics, antibiotics, antipsychotics), electrolyte abnormalities (hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypocalcaemia).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','easy','mcq','In the fetal circulation, oxygenated blood from the placenta travels to the fetus via the:','["A. Umbilical artery", "B. Ductus venosus directly to the heart", "C. Umbilical vein", "D. Portal vein"]','C','Oxygenated blood from the placenta returns to the fetus via the umbilical vein (single vessel), which carries blood with the highest fetal oxygen saturation (~80%). The umbilical arteries (two vessels) carry deoxygenated blood back to the placenta. This is the reverse of the postnatal convention - the umbilical vein is the exception where a vein carries oxygenated blood.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','medium','mcq','The ductus venosus in the fetus allows:','["A. Blood to bypass the lungs from pulmonary artery to aorta", "B. Blood to flow from the left to right atrium", "C. Blood to bypass the ductus arteriosus", "D. Oxygenated portal blood to bypass the liver and enter the inferior vena cava directly"]','D','The ductus venosus is a fetal shunt connecting the umbilical vein directly to the inferior vena cava (IVC), allowing approximately 50% of placental oxygenated blood to bypass the liver sinusoids. This delivers highly oxygenated blood rapidly to the right atrium and then preferentially through the foramen ovale to the left side of the heart and brain. The ductus venosus closes after birth, becoming the ligamentum venosum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','medium','mcq','Which of the following correctly describes the foramen ovale in the fetal heart?','["A. An opening in the interatrial septum allowing blood to flow from right atrium to left atrium", "B. A shunt between the pulmonary artery and aorta", "C. A shunt bypassing the liver", "D. A connection between the umbilical artery and aorta"]','A','The foramen ovale is an opening in the interatrial septum (within the fossa ovalis) through which blood flows from the right atrium to the left atrium in the fetus. This preferentially directs the most oxygenated blood (from the IVC) to the left heart and then to the coronary arteries and brain. In fetal life, high pulmonary vascular resistance keeps right-sided pressures high, maintaining the right-to-left shunt.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','easy','mcq','The ductus arteriosus connects which two vessels in the fetal circulation?','["A. Aorta to the inferior vena cava", "B. Pulmonary artery to the aorta", "C. Umbilical vein to the portal vein", "D. Superior vena cava to the pulmonary vein"]','B','The ductus arteriosus connects the pulmonary artery (at its bifurcation) to the descending aorta. In the fetus, because pulmonary vascular resistance is very high (fluid-filled lungs), most right ventricular output is diverted through the ductus arteriosus to the systemic circulation, bypassing the lungs. After birth, it normally closes within 24-72 hours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','medium','mcq','Immediately after birth, which ONE event is MOST critical in initiating closure of the foramen ovale?','["A. Clamping of the umbilical cord increases systemic resistance", "B. The rise in body temperature stimulates atrial contraction", "C. The first breath expands the lungs, reducing pulmonary vascular resistance and reversing the atrial pressure gradient", "D. Prostaglandin release causes smooth muscle contraction in the interatrial septum"]','C','With the first breath: (1) alveoli expand and fill with air; (2) pulmonary vascular resistance falls dramatically; (3) pulmonary blood flow increases, returning more blood to the left atrium; (4) left atrial pressure exceeds right atrial pressure; (5) the flap valve of the foramen ovale is pushed shut and ultimately fuses. Clamping the cord removes the low-resistance placenta, increasing SVR and reinforcing this pressure reversal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','hard','mcq','Indomethacin is used to close a patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) in a premature infant. What is its mechanism of action?','["A. Direct smooth muscle stimulation causing ductal spasm", "B. Beta-adrenoceptor blockade reducing ductal blood flow", "C. Increasing pulmonary vascular resistance to reduce ductal shunt", "D. Inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis (COX inhibitor), removing PGE2-mediated ductal dilation"]','D','The ductus arteriosus is maintained OPEN in fetal life primarily by high circulating prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) and low oxygen tension. At birth, oxygen tension rises and prostaglandin levels fall, causing ductal smooth muscle constriction and closure. In prematurity, PGE2 levels remain high, keeping the DA open. Indomethacin (COX-1/2 inhibitor) blocks prostaglandin synthesis, allowing the DA to close. Surgical ligation is used if medical therapy fails.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','medium','mcq','Fetal haemoglobin (HbF) differs from adult haemoglobin (HbA) in that HbF:','["A. Has lower affinity for 2,3-DPG, giving it higher oxygen affinity and facilitating placental oxygen transfer", "B. Has higher affinity for 2,3-DPG, releasing oxygen more readily to tissues", "C. Has lower oxygen carrying capacity than HbA", "D. Has the same oxygen dissociation curve as HbA"]','A','HbF (alpha-2 gamma-2) binds 2,3-DPG less avidly than HbA (alpha-2 beta-2), because gamma chains lack the key histidine residue. This gives HbF a higher oxygen affinity (leftward shift of ODC) compared to maternal HbA. At the placenta, maternal blood releases oxygen to fetal blood because HbF captures oxygen from maternal HbA, facilitating transplacental oxygen transfer.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','hard','mcq','A cyanotic newborn has parallel circulation with the aorta arising from the right ventricle and pulmonary artery from the left ventricle (transposition of the great arteries). Which fetal shunts must remain open for survival?','["A. A VSD only", "B. Patent ductus arteriosus and/or foramen ovale must remain open to allow mixing", "C. Ductus venosus only", "D. Aortic valve and pulmonary valve"]','B','In transposition of the great arteries (TGA), two parallel circuits exist - deoxygenated blood circulates through the systemic circulation and oxygenated blood circulates through the pulmonary circulation without mixing. Survival depends on maintaining the fetal shunts: foramen ovale (ASD), patent ductus arteriosus (PDA), or a VSD. Prostaglandin E1 infusion is given immediately to keep the PDA open; urgent balloon atrial septostomy (Rashkind procedure) or arterial switch operation is performed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','medium','mcq','Which fetal structure becomes the ligamentum arteriosum after birth?','["A. Ductus venosus", "B. Foramen ovale", "C. Ductus arteriosus", "D. Umbilical vein"]','C','After birth, the ductus arteriosus (connecting the pulmonary artery to the aorta) closes functionally within 24-72 hours and anatomically within 2-3 weeks, becoming the ligamentum arteriosum. Other postnatal remnants: ductus venosus becomes the ligamentum venosum; umbilical vein becomes the ligamentum teres hepatis; umbilical arteries become medial umbilical ligaments; foramen ovale becomes the fossa ovalis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Cardiovascular System','Anatomy & Physiology','Fetal Circulation & Changes at Birth','easy','mcq','What happens to pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) immediately after birth?','["A. PVR rises sharply due to increased blood flow", "B. PVR remains unchanged for the first week", "C. PVR gradually falls over months only", "D. PVR falls dramatically with the first breath as lungs expand and alveolar PO2 rises"]','D','In utero, the fetal lungs are fluid-filled, collapsed, and hypoxic - this causes profound pulmonary vasoconstriction (hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction), making PVR very high (approximately 8x systemic). With the first breath, alveoli expand, alveolar PO2 rises, hypoxic vasoconstriction is relieved, and PVR falls dramatically and rapidly (within minutes to hours). This increases pulmonary blood flow 8-fold, fundamentally changing the circulatory pattern to the neonatal configuration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
